## 5. Remove Element

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int removeElement(vector<int>& nums, int val) {
        int k=0;
        for(int i=0;i<nums.size();i++){
            if(nums[i]!=val){
                nums[k]=nums[i];
                k++;
            }
        }
        return k;
    }
};
```

## 6. Remove Duplicates from Sorted Array

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int removeDuplicates(vector<int>& nums) {
        if(nums.size()==0) return 0;
        int k=1;
        for(int i=1;i<nums.size();i++){
            if(nums[i]!=nums[i-1]){
                nums[k]=nums[i];
                k++;
            }
        }
        return k;
    }
};
```

## 7. Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int maxProfit(vector<int>& prices) {
        int minPrice=INT_MAX;
        int profit=0;
        for(int price:prices){
            minPrice=min(minPrice,price);
            profit=max(profit,price-minPrice);
        }
        return profit;
    }
};
```

## 8. Contains Duplicate

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    bool containsDuplicate(vector<int>& nums) {
        unordered_set<int> st;
        for(int num:nums){
            if(st.count(num))
                return true;
            st.insert(num);
        }
        return false;
    }
};
```

## 9. Maximum Subarray

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int maxSubArray(vector<int>& nums) {
        int curr=nums[0];
        int maxi=nums[0];
        for(int i=1;i<nums.size();i++){
            curr=max(nums[i],curr+nums[i]);
            maxi=max(maxi,curr);
        }
        return maxi;
    }
};
```

## 10. Subtract the Product and Sum of Digits of an Integer

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int subtractProductAndSum(int n) {
        int product=1,sum=0;
        while(n){
            int digit=n%10;
            product*=digit;
            sum+=digit;
            n/=10;
        }
        return product-sum;
    }
};
```
# LeetCode 191: Number of 1 Bits (Hamming Weight)

## Problem Statement

Given a positive integer `n`, write a function that returns the number of set bits (`1`s) in its binary representation.

### Example

```text
Input: n = 11

Binary Representation: 1011

Output: 3
```

---

## C++ Solution

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int hammingWeight(uint32_t n) {
        int count = 0;

        while (n != 0) {
            n &= (n - 1);
            ++count;
        }

        return count;
    }
};
```

---

## Approach: Brian Kernighan's Algorithm

The expression:

```cpp
n & (n - 1)
```

removes the **rightmost set bit (1)** from the binary representation of `n`.

### Example

```text
n = 12

Binary: 1100

n - 1 = 1011

1100
1011
----
1000
```

The last set bit is removed.

---

## Dry Run

For `n = 11`:

```text
11 = 1011
```

### Iteration 1

```text
1011 & 1010 = 1010
Count = 1
```

### Iteration 2

```text
1010 & 1001 = 1000
Count = 2
```

### Iteration 3

```text
1000 & 0111 = 0000
Count = 3
```

Loop terminates because `n = 0`.

### Final Answer

```text
3
```

---

## Time Complexity

* **O(k)**, where `k` is the number of set bits.

## Space Complexity

* **O(1)**

---

## Key Learning

> `n & (n - 1)` removes the rightmost set bit from a number.

This technique is widely used in bit manipulation problems and is known as **Brian Kernighan's Algorithm**.

